<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">About encryption</div>
		<h1>Encrypted</h1>
		<p class="lede">Full-disk encryption with an on-screen keyboard at boot. It
		works on this phone, and there is no image to download.</p>
		<p>Like every route here, it needs the phone unlocked at Xiaomi first
		&mdash; a Mi account and seven days of waiting.
		<a href="unlock.html">The steps</a>.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>The phone does it, on the first boot</h2></div>
		<p>Flash the <a href="install-image.html">ordinary image</a>. On the first
		boot, right after choosing a password, the phone asks: <b>encrypt this
		phone?</b> Say yes and choose a passphrase. It restarts, and before anything
		is mounted the initramfs encrypts the storage in place. From then on every
		start asks for the passphrase on the screen, before the PIN.</p>
		<p>That is the whole route. No PC is involved in the encryption, nothing on
		your computer needs root, and there is no image to build. A LUKS master key
		is made when the volume is formatted, and here that happens on the phone
		that will use it &mdash; the only place it should ever exist. It is also why
		no encrypted image will ever be offered for download: one made on somebody
		else's machine would carry a key that machine had seen.</p>
		<p>Plug the charger in first; it takes a while and it says so. If the battery
		is low and there is no charger it postpones itself and tells you. If the
		phone dies halfway, the next boot asks for the passphrase and finishes.
		&ldquo;Not now&rdquo; is for good; it can still be done later from a
		terminal, with the same script the first boot uses.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>02</span><h2>What it costs</h2></div>
		<p>Encrypting the whole partition takes a while &mdash; the phone reads and
		writes every sector once. Twenty to forty minutes is normal, and the screen
		shows a percentage the whole time. <b>Plug the charger in and do not turn it
		off.</b> If it does die halfway, the next boot asks for the passphrase and
		finishes what was left; nothing is lost.</p>
		<p>The passphrase cannot be recovered. Forget it and the phone is a brick
		with your data inside &mdash; there is no back door and no reset that keeps the
		data.</p>
		<p><b>&ldquo;Not now&rdquo; is for good:</b> the question does not come back. You
		can still encrypt later from a terminal on the phone, which runs the same
		thing the first boot would have:</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON THE PHONE</span></div>
			<pre>printf 'your passphrase' | pkexec /usr/libexec/utsugi-surya/request-encryption</pre>
		</div>
		<p>It writes the passphrase where the initramfs reads it and restarts, exactly
		as the assistant does.</p>

